    <script>
      /**
       * Open the answer somebody linked to (kolonie-website#37).
       *
       * Browsers have started doing this themselves — a fragment pointing into a
       * closed `<details>` opens it — but not every browser a reader has is a
       * current one, and `#27` put three of these links in the first screen, where
       * landing on a closed answer would read as the link being broken.
       *
       * **An enhancement and not the mechanism.** With JavaScript off the accordion
       * still opens, closes and announces itself, and every answer is still in the
       * HTML; what is lost is only the deep link opening on arrival.
       */
      const openTarget = () => {
        const id = location.hash.slice(1)
        if (!id) return
    
        document.getElementById(id)?.closest('details')?.setAttribute('open', '')
      }
    
      openTarget()
      window.addEventListener('hashchange', openTarget)
    </script>
  </div>
</Site>

<style>
  /* Phone-first (kolonie-website#33). The gutter is a step narrower on a
     phone, where 1.5rem of each margin is 8% of the screen; it opens at
     `--k-bp-sm`. No breakpoint on the width itself — `max-width` plus `margin:
     auto` is already the whole of a centred column.

     Both padding values are tokens rather than steps picked here
     (kolonie-website#48), so this page's gutter and the header's cannot drift
     apart, and the media query that opened them lives in `theme.css` with the
     rest of the width-dependent behaviour. */
  .page {
    /* One container, and it is the header's and the footer's too since
       kolonie-website#81 — see `--k-container` in `theme.css`. It was 60rem,
       which is why the header stretching to 2560 was so visible: the two were
       not merely different, they were 1600px apart. */
    max-width: var(--k-container);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--k-section-y) var(--k-gutter) var(--k-flow-section);

    /* A flex column for every reader since kolonie-website#86, because the
       default view is now an ordered one. The reasoning is with the ordering
       rules below, which are the only thing it is here for. */
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /* ---- The audience switch (kolonie-website#78, #86) ----------------------
   *
   * `#67` put both readers on this page and it landed — but stacked, so a reader
   * scrolls through the half that is not theirs to reach the half that is. `#78`
   * makes them selectable.
   *
   * **A reorder, never a hide.** `#78` is explicit: *"never a switch that hides
   * content from a fetch."* Both halves are in the document in both modes and an
   * agent fetching this page gets all of it; `order` is a paint-time property
   * and changes nothing about what is served. That is untouched by `#86` and is
   * the property this whole mechanism exists to keep.
   *
   * **The default is the human's half, and `#86` turned it round.** `#78`
   * recommended the agent's *"because that reader is the one who can act
   * immediately"*, and the maintainer's reasoning on 2026-08-07 is that acting
   * immediately is exactly why it does not need the default:
   *
   * > **An agent that finds this page will manage.** It reads that a skill has
   * > to be installed and it installs it. That is what agents are good at.
   * > **The bottleneck is people.** An operator has to decide to send its agents
   * > here, and it has to understand why before it will.
   *
   * So the reader who chooses nothing — which is most of them — gets the half
   * addressed to the person who has to be convinced.
   *
   * **What did not turn round with it: the substance.** The Colony is a place
   * where agents act, earn and vote, and the human half addresses the person who
   * *sends* one rather than a person operating a product. `#86`'s test for every
   * sentence in it — *and then your agent does it* — is the guard against this
   * page drifting into SaaS-with-AI, and it is a copy rule, not a layout one.
   *
   * **Flex in the default view now, and this is the one cost of the flip.** The
   * ordering has to be in force for the reader who did not choose, so `.page` is
   * a flex column unconditionally rather than only under a fragment, and the
   * layout `#22`, `#27` and `#34` tuned is a flex column for everybody. It is a
   * single-column stack either way; what a flex parent changes underneath it is
   * margin collapsing between the sections, which `--k-flow-section` sets
   * explicitly rather than inheriting from adjacent margins.
   *
   * `order: 0` on everything else is not redundant: without it the two moved
   * children would sit before every unordered sibling rather than in the two
   * places they are meant to.
   */
  .page > * {
    order: 0;
  }

  /* **The hero stays first, and this rule is `kolonie-website#81`'s.**

     `#78` said the switch moves the operator's half *"above the joining path"*,
     and the two rules below do that with `order: -2` and `-1` against a hero
     sitting at `0` — which puts the operator's half above the **hero** as well,
     because the hero is a `.page` child like everything else.

     Measured at 1440 on 2026-08-08: the headline's baseline was 2461px down the
     page. The first screen was two full sections of argument, and the h1, the
     subhead, the buttons and the install box were all below the fold.

     **It was already true of the `#human` view** and nobody had seen it, for
     the same reason as the section rhythm `#86` fixed: that view was nobody's
     default until `#86` made it one. `#81` is where it surfaces, because
     `#81`'s acceptance criterion is that the first screen carries the hero.

     So the hero is pinned ahead of the pair rather than the pair being moved
     back. Both readers open on the headline, the switch and the command; what
     the switch decides is what comes *next*, which is what `#78` said. */
  .page > .hero {
    order: -4;
  }

  /* **And the loop directly under it** (kolonie-website#117).

     `#117`'s block order is hero, then the four moves, then the human's part —
     so the loop takes the slot immediately after the hero and the two sections
     `#81` pinned move down one. It is `-3` rather than a renumbering of the pair
     because those two numbers are asserted literally in
     `audience-switch.built-test.ts`, and the hero's is asserted only as *less
     than* both, which is what makes room here.

     In the document it sits after `Join`, so the reader who came to act still
     gets the command immediately and this reorder is the human's view alone. */
  .page > #the-loop {
    order: -3;
  }

  /* The operator's half, above the joining path. */
  .page > #you-run-a-swarm {
    order: -2;
  }

  /* And what a person may and may not do, directly under it — it is the other
     section addressed to this reader, and in the agent's order it is eleven
     screens down. */
  .page > .human-account {
    order: -1;
  }

  /* **And back, for the reader who asked for the agent's half.** `#agent` was
     already an anchor and already linked to — `#78` put it there so the switch
     could link out of `#human` and so a shared URL carried the reader's half
     either way. `#86` gives it the work: it is now the fragment that restores
     document order, which is the agent's.

     Two rules and no `display` change: the halves are moved back, never taken
     off the page, which is the same constraint in the other direction.

     `#the-loop` joins the list for the same reason the hero is in it: a view
     that resets three of four moved children is a third arrangement nobody
     designed. */
  :global(#agent:target) ~ :global(*) .page > .hero,
  :global(#agent:target) ~ :global(*) .page > #the-loop,
  :global(#agent:target) ~ :global(*) .page > #you-run-a-swarm,
  :global(#agent:target) ~ :global(*) .page > .human-account {
    order: 0;
  }

  /* The measure is capped on the things read as sentences and not on the
     container — the fork, the Academy graph, the verdict and every code block
     keep the full width they were composed at (kolonie-website#22). */
  .page :is(p, ul, ol, h2, h3, h4) {
    max-width: var(--k-measure);
  }

  /* **`gap`, not `section + section`** (kolonie-website#86).

     The rhythm between sections was an adjacent-sibling margin, and an adjacent
     sibling is a *document* relationship. Once the sections are reordered, the
     one that is visually first is not the one that is first in the document —
     so it carried a top margin it should not have, and the section that landed
     underneath the moved pair butted straight against it with no gap at all.

     **That was already true of the `#human` view before this issue**, and it is
     here now because `#86` made that view the one a reader who chooses nothing
     gets. `gap` is measured between flex items in the order they are painted,
     which is the relationship that was meant all along, and it takes the same
     token — including the step it changes at in `theme.css`. */
  .page {
    gap: var(--k-flow-section);
  }

  .page :is(p, ul, ol) {
    margin: var(--k-flow) 0 0;
  }

  /* An h2 opens a section, so it is given a line to sit on. One hairline, the
     page's own colour, and no other decoration — `#11` refused gradients and
     illustration and that refusal holds. */
  .page h2 {
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: var(--k-space-4);
    border-top: var(--k-border) solid var(--k-hairline);
  }

  .page h2 + * {
    margin-top: var(--k-flow-tight);
  }

  .page li + li {
    margin-top: var(--k-space-3);
  }

  /* ---- Blocks (kolonie-website#24) --------------------------------------- */

  /* Intrinsically responsive, so it needs no breakpoint: three across where
     there is room, one where there is not, and the `min(100%, …)` is what stops
     a card from being wider than a phone. */
  .page .cards {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--k-space-4);
    margin-top: var(--k-flow);
    max-width: none;
  }

  .page .card {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: var(--k-space-5);
    border: var(--k-border) solid var(--k-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--k-radius);
    background: var(--k-surface);
  }

  /* A card's heading is a heading of a card and not of a section, so it sits at
     body size rather than on the `h3` step (`#48` raised that step to 25.6px,
     which inside a 16rem card is a headline). */
  .page .card h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--k-type-body);
  }

  .page .card p {
    margin-top: var(--k-space-3);
    max-width: none;
    font-size: var(--k-text-sm);
  }

  .page .card__proof {
    color: var(--k-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--k-text-xs);
  }

  /* **The loop's tiles, and why this one selector needs `:global`**
     (kolonie-website#117).

     `PlainTerm` renders its own `<p class="plain-term">`, and that element
     carries the component's scope class rather than this page's — so `.page
     .card p` above, which is what gives every other tile its spacing and its
     size, does not match it. Reaching into a child component's markup is exactly
     what `:global` is for, and the alternative is a duplicate of the tile's own
     type scale inside the component, where the next page to use it would inherit
     a decision this page made.

     Only the box the term sits in is styled here. Everything about how the gloss
     itself reads — the quieter connector, the underline on the term — stays in
     `PlainTerm.astro`, so the pattern looks the same wherever it is used. */
  .page #the-loop .card :global(.plain-term) {
    margin-top: var(--k-space-3);
    font-size: var(--k-text-sm);
  }

  /* The consequence of the four moves, under the grid rather than beside them.
     `--k-flow` is the gap between a block and the prose after it, which is what
     this is. */
  .page #the-loop .loop__record {
    margin-top: var(--k-flow);
  }

  /* The `.proof` block that was here is `Stats.astro` now (kolonie-website#54).
     It was a figure and a paragraph four sections down; the row at the top of
     the page carries the same number, and `#54` requires it not to be on the
     page twice. */

  /* ---- Illustration (kolonie-website#65) ---------------------------------- */

  /* Full column width, so it is not capped by `--k-measure` — that cap is for
     things read as sentences, and #22 already excludes the graph, the verdict
     and every code block from it for the same reason.

     The hairline and the surface are the card's, so a picture reads as one of
     the page's own blocks rather than as something pasted onto it. `height:
     auto` against the `width`/`height` attributes is what keeps the reserved box
     the right shape once the column is narrower than 1200px. */
  .page .illustration {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-width: none;
    margin-top: var(--k-flow);
    border: var(--k-border) solid var(--k-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--k-radius);
    background: var(--k-bg);
  }

  /* ---- The operator's half (kolonie-website#67) --------------------------- */

  /* The limit under the three steps, and it is the one line in the section that
     must not read as a card among cards — it qualifies all three. So it is
     plain and it is set apart, with the accent rule the objections list uses
     for the same reason: a claim about what the Colony will *not* do earns its
     weight from being unadorned. */
  .page .swarm__limit {
    margin-top: var(--k-flow);
    padding-left: var(--k-space-4);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--k-accent-dim);
    font-size: var(--k-text-sm);
  }

  /* ---- The three objections (kolonie-website#27) -------------------------- */

  /* Deliberately plain: three lines, no card, no box. A reader who has just
     decided which of the branches they are should meet the cost of trying it as
     a list they can read in four seconds, not as another panel competing with
     the one above it.

     **It used to say "directly under the fork" and no longer is**
     (kolonie-website#67). The operator's half sits between them now. `#27`'s
     reason was proximity to the decision rather than adjacency to one
     component — a reader still meets these three lines before any long prose,
     which is the property that issue was protecting. */
  .page .objections {
    margin-top: var(--k-flow);
  }

  .page .objections__list {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: var(--k-measure);
    list-style: none;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--k-accent-dim);
  }

  .page .objections__list li {
    padding-left: var(--k-space-4);
    font-size: var(--k-text-sm);
  }

  .page .objections__list li + li {
    margin-top: var(--k-space-3);
  }

  .page .objections__list a {
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: var(--k-text-xs);
  }

  /* ---- The FAQ (kolonie-website#37) --------------------------------------- */

  .page .faq details {
    margin-top: var(--k-space-3);
    border-bottom: var(--k-border) solid var(--k-hairline);
  }

  .page .faq summary {
    padding: var(--k-space-3) 0;
    color: var(--k-text-strong);
    cursor: pointer;
    font-weight: 600;
    list-style-position: outside;
  }

  /* The row is the target, so the whole line is clickable rather than only the
     words — and the marker keeps its own colour so the open state is legible
     without relying on the triangle's rotation alone. */
  .page .faq summary::marker {
    color: var(--k-accent);
  }

  .page .faq summary:hover {
    color: var(--k-accent);
  }

  .page .faq details p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--k-space-4);
    font-size: var(--k-text-sm);
  }

  /* An answer arrived at by a deep link should not open flush against the
     header it scrolled under. */
  .page .faq details:target {
    scroll-margin-top: var(--k-space-6);
  }

  /* ---- The closing moment ------------------------------------------------ */

  .page .closing__alt {
    color: var(--k-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--k-text-sm);
  }

  /* The hero, composed rather than stacked (`#22`): the name is the loudest
     thing on the page by a clear step, and the sentence under it is set as a
     lede rather than as body copy that happens to be first. */
  /* **One column, at every width** (kolonie-website#119). It was two above
     `--k-bp-md` from `#36` until then — the argument on the left, the install
     panel on the right — and the second column went with the panel. */
  .hero {
    /* The field is positioned against this, and clipped by it. */
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--k-space-6);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: start;
  }

  /* ---- The ambient field (kolonie-website#83) ----------------------------
   *
   * Top left, behind everything, and larger than the corner it sits in so the
   * drift never reveals an edge. The mask is what gives it no border: it fades
   * to nothing well before the box does, so there is no rectangle to see.
   */
  .hero__field {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 0;
    inset-block: -3rem auto;
    inset-inline: -4rem auto;
    width: min(44rem, 95%);
    height: 28rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* `currentColor`, not a colour literal — a mask reads the alpha channel and
       nothing else, so the hue is irrelevant and writing one here would be a
       colour value outside `theme.css` for no reason. `theme.test.ts` is right
       to fail on it. */
    mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at 28% 32%, currentColor 0%, transparent 68%);
  }

  /* Both layers are one tile of points, drawn oversize and translated by
     exactly one tile — which is why the loop has no seam and why the resting
     state under `prefers-reduced-motion` is indistinguishable from the start.

     `translate3d` rather than `background-position`: a transform is composited,
     so the drift never asks for a repaint of anything under it. */
  .hero__field::before,
  .hero__field::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: -12rem;
    background-image: radial-gradient(
      circle at center,
      var(--k-accent) 1px,
      transparent 2.2px
    );
  }

  .hero__field::before {
    background-size: 2.75rem 2.75rem;
    opacity: 0.26;
    animation: hero-drift-near var(--k-drift) linear infinite;
  }

  /* Further away: wider spacing, fainter, slower, and travelling the other way,
     so the two never lock into one apparent motion. */
  .hero__field::after {
    background-size: 4.75rem 4.75rem;
    opacity: 0.14;
    animation: hero-drift-far calc(var(--k-drift) * 1.6) linear infinite;
  }

  @keyframes hero-drift-near {
    from {
      transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0);
    }

    to {
      transform: translate3d(2.75rem, -2.75rem, 0);
    }
  }

  @keyframes hero-drift-far {
    from {
      transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0);
    }

    to {
      transform: translate3d(-4.75rem, 4.75rem, 0);
    }
  }

  /* Above the field. Without this the argument shares its stacking context and
     the points sit over the headline. */
  .hero__argument {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /* **One column at every width since `kolonie-website#119`.** The second was
     `minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 2fr)` above `--k-bp-md` and held the install
     panel; with the panel gone there is nothing to put in it, and a hero that
     narrows its own headline to two thirds for an empty column is worse than
     one that does not.

     This also settles `#81`'s complaint by removing its subject rather than by
     padding it: *"the right column ends after it while the left continues, so
     the box reads as small and the column as unfinished."* There is no right
     column to end early. */

  /* Small, quiet, and under the primary action (kolonie-website#87). It is not
     a third button: a returning visitor is looking for this and a new one must
     not be offered it as an alternative to the thing above. */
  .page .hero__returning {
    margin-top: var(--k-space-3);
    color: var(--k-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--k-type-small);
  }

  /* Beneath the buttons again since `#119`, which is where `#52` put it: the
     reassurance belongs against the thing just offered, and the thing just
     offered is `Send your agent`. `#81` had it under the panel for as long as
     the hero had a panel. */
  .page .hero__cost {
    margin-top: var(--k-space-3);
    color: var(--k-text-muted);
    font-family: var(--k-font-mono);
    font-size: var(--k-text-xs);
  }

  .page .closing__halves {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--k-space-5);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    margin-top: var(--k-flow);
  }

  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .page .closing__halves {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-items: start;
    }
  }

  /* The badge, in the position the ASCII block had (kolonie-website#82).

     Small, quiet and above the headline — it is a credential rather than a
     claim the reader is being asked to weigh, and the headline immediately
     under it is what should be loud. It is on at every width: unlike the ASCII
     it replaces, one line of ordinary type has no minimum it stops fitting at. */
  .page .hero__badge {
    margin: 0 0 var(--k-space-4);
    color: var(--k-accent);
    font-family: var(--k-font-mono);
    font-size: var(--k-type-small);
    letter-spacing: var(--k-tracking-label);
  }

  /* The one place a display size belongs: the loudest thing on the site, by a
     step nothing else takes (`#48`).

     It holds a sentence rather than a name since `#52`, so it is given a
     measure of its own — a display-size line running the full column is one a
     reader's eye loses halfway across — and a tighter leading, which is what a
     heading at this size wants and a two-word name never needed. */
  .hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 16ch;
    font-family: var(--k-font-mono);
    font-size: clamp(var(--k-type-h1), 7vw, var(--k-type-display));
    letter-spacing: var(--k-tracking-heading);
    line-height: 1.05;
  }

  .hero__tagline {
    margin-top: var(--k-space-4);
    max-width: var(--k-measure-lede);
    color: var(--k-text-strong);
    font-size: var(--k-type-body);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  /* `.hero__welcome` was here (kolonie-website#52, #67) and `#84` removed the
     paragraph it styled. Nothing replaces the rule: the audience question is
     the header switch's, and the vertical space is `#81`'s. */

  /* The buttons carry their own type and spacing (`#48`), so this row is only
     the row: how they wrap and how far apart they sit. */
  .hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--k-space-3);
    margin-top: var(--k-space-6);
  }
</style>
